On Tue, 9 Aug 2005 dogscoff@eudoramail.com wrote:

> what if there were a way to harness the productivity of ppl coding for a
> living, while retaining the free, open-source nature of the game?

Usually, this works in free software products if the company wants to sell
hardware, wants to sell support for the product or wants to drive an
opponent out of a market. I do not see any of this happening; except,
maybe, that a manufacturer of a mobile computing device might port NetHack
to it to increase the sales of the product. But even this is highly
unlikely, because we are few and far between.

> Businesses have really picked up on online advertising in the last few
> years.

Online advertisement is tolerated because users understand that some
institutions need the income to uphold a particular service. This is not
the case for NetHack.

> I wonder if companies would be interested in using software like nethack
> to advertise.

Not for such a small audience as NetHackers.

> Let's take nethack's much-YANIed "photography patch". While there is at
> least one working photographry patch, it would suit my example better
> if there wasn't. Suppose there was no photography patch- why couldn't a
> company like (for example) Nikon write one? A technology firm like that
> ought to have lots of competent IT people kicking around that would be
> capable of undertaking such a task.

Do these people know what we want a patch to do? Do they understand all
implications? Do they "think of everything"?

> They could rename the "expensive camera" to "a Nikon camera" and write
> the patch. Release it to the public and hey look- instant geek-cred. We
> get our patch, they get an image-boost, everyone's happy.

Let's say they add the camera as another item. Furthermore, let's say
NVIDIA releases OpenGL accelerated 3d-NetHack. NV-Hack does not have the
graphics to represent Nikon's camera. And now? Currently, NetHack patch
makers are usually aware of other patches and are interested in
interoperability.

> The patches produced by advertisers would be entirely optional- if ppl
> didn't like them , they'd simply go back to playing without the patch.

Patches are likely to introduce new items. This means that bones
compatibility is not preserved, saved file compatibility is not preserved,
interface compatibility is not preserved, and it is harder to make patches
work together. So it is not easily possible to just switch back and forth
between various patch sets. In the end, we get plain NetHack and maybe
something like PatchHack, offering a selected collection of patches
working together flawlessly. It is not so simple to add just this patch,
and this, and something of this,..., and have something that compiles,
much less something that produces a balanced game.

> What's more, the patch would (ideally) be open-source so it would be
> relatively simple for someone to alter the code and remove the bits they
> didn't like.

So if someone decides that the Nikon camera (or something else) is
overpowered and removes something from its powers for balance reasons,
shall this still be called the Nikon patch? Would Nikon allow this? No.
Thus, Nikon would not release the patch under the NetHack license.

> 1- Product twinking. Manufacturer X adds a cool new item to the game.
> Manufaturer Yhas a similar idea, but they have to make their item more
> powerful and cool than manufacturer X's item.

Any cases of patches from X and Y (where X!=Y) playing together is a
problem.

> So... who's first with the comments/ suggestions/ flames/ hysterical
> rants?

I'm the second to answer, sort me in any of these categories.

